#{[{:activity/expected-seconds 420.0 :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120732} :activity/id #uuid "d014fcfb-88d3-58d4-928d-09436eaced72" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048218 :topic/id #uuid "893a2f73-ff77-57dc-a5aa-12aa27975a06" :topic/math-academy-id 1866} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120732 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186121056 :tutorial/content "There is something interesting about the bases of a vector space, summarized in the theorem below:\n\nIf the vector space $V$ has a basis with $n$ vectors, then any basis of $V$ has exactly $n$ vectors.\n\nThe number $n$ of vectors in any basis of $V$ is called the **dimension of the space** $V$ and is denoted $\\textrm{dim}(V).$\n\nFor example, consider the linear span below:\n![](images/3f/3f4139a1701c74e72ca55b662b1d5c06b02f1d3b6cab9a96b4667439dc196068.svg)\n\nWe know that, in order to find a basis of the span, we start by creating a matrix whose columns are made up of our vectors, and then we reduce the matrix to row echelon form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\begin{bmatrix}3 & -3 & -6 \\\\ -1 & 1 & 5\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+\\frac{1}{3}{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}3 & -3 & -6 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 3\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe see that the matrix above has two pivot columns: the $1$st and the $3$rd columns. This tells us that\n$\\mathcal{B}=\\{ \\mathbf{v}_1,\\mathbf{v}_3 \\}$ is a basis of $H.$\n\nSince $\\mathcal{B}$ consists of two vectors, *any basis* of $H$ will also have *exactly* two vectors. So, we have $\\textrm{dim}(H)=2.$" :tutorial/id #uuid "6700cc12-77d9-59b2-8ee8-826575be0c12" :tutorial/math-academy-id 3517 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "f0eb1a4d-9c4f-513f-be21-c3f099b4b267" :step/math-academy-id 19970 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120762}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120733 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119845 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119659 :question/id #uuid "b36a943c-f00a-5436-9256-0725a0008fdb" :question/math-academy-id "e-5410"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "8a5d11c2-4a7c-5fdc-a363-8a87b90db19e" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049222}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120529} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120530} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278261} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186348198} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186348199} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186348201} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186348202} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186348203} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186348204} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186348205} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186410545} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186410546} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186424595} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186430827} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186430829} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186483398}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Dimension of a Span by Inspection"} :step/id #uuid "c96304c7-f6d5-5dfd-b33f-9c76165825dd" :step/math-academy-id 19971 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120734}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120734 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119846 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119660 :question/id #uuid "ef028d89-e645-5ef3-a881-092292e2a271" :question/math-academy-id "e-5411"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "004c4177-ca0e-586c-962a-1220b4b64f47" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120365} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120367} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186319174} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186321058} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186321061} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186321062} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186321063} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186321065} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186410547} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186410548} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186424594} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186430828} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186481376}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Dimension of a Span Using Row Reduction"} :step/id #uuid "aa44487c-2db5-5c3c-bc3c-c62ca070f704" :step/math-academy-id 19972 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120735}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120735 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186121057 :tutorial/content "We can build some geometric intuition for finite-dimensional vector spaces. To illustrate, let's find the dimensions of $\\{\\mathbf{0}\\},$ $\\mathbb{R},$ $\\mathbb{R}^2,$ and $\\mathbb{R}^3.$\n\nFirst, note that $\\{\\mathbf{0}\\}$ can be written as $\\mathbb{R}^0,$ and $\\mathbb{R}$ can be written as $\\mathbb{R}^1.$ We have the following standard bases:\n\n![](images/44/44f00659592bc6ac2742af0105fa493efa93e58e8dd63ee9af56a2d90b0e513a.svg)\n\nConsequently, we have the following dimensions:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{dim}({R}^{0})=0,\\,\\text{dim}({R}^{1})=1,\\,\\text{dim}({R}^{2})=2,\\,\\text{dim}({R}^{3})=3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let's build some geometric intuition. We can represent the standard bases in the following diagram:\n\n![](images/16/169b5597d89fa32fd09772593cc2a9a330ee12881281dea4e3e43dc6165b9ebd.png)\n\nSo, the sequence $\\mathbb{R}^0,$ $\\mathbb{R}^1,$ $\\mathbb{R}^2,$ $\\mathbb{R}^3,$ $\\ldots$ can be visualized as follows:\n\n![](images/37/379823eca26d43337744a8f4a3872e67ac537a6c193d55e39b5d6761f258f6f5.png)\n\nNotice the following:\n\n- The basis for $\\mathbb{R}^0$ is represented geometrically as a $0$-dimensional point.\n\n- The basis for $\\mathbb{R}^1$ is represented geometrically as a $1$-dimensional line segment.\n\n- The basis for $\\mathbb{R}^2$ is represented geometrically as a $2$-dimensional square.\n\n- The basis for $\\mathbb{R}^3$ is represented geometrically as a $3$-dimensional cube.\n\nUnfortunately, we can't visualize dimensions greater than $3,$ but we know that $\\mathbb{R}^n$ has the following standard basis consisting of $n$ vectors:\n\n![](images/c8/c8a606f297f86b56224cb9f50ba68b826e614d4f075b396a4104e19bc77c46e7.svg)\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n\\textrm{dim}(\\mathbb{R}^n) = n.\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "88c2cca4-9e7b-5ace-b239-798bcab0d26a" :tutorial/math-academy-id 3518 :tutorial/title "Geometric Intuition for Finite-Dimensional Vector Spaces"} :step/id #uuid "c692fd19-bc87-5c2c-a172-5d1d8ce64f90" :step/math-academy-id 19974} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120762 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186121069 :tutorial/content "As defined earlier, the dimension of a vector space $V$ is the number of vectors in any basis of $V$.\n\nHowever, note that the definition above is only applicable if a vector space has at least one basis with a finite number of elements.\n\n- If a vector space has a basis with a finite number of vectors, then it is called **finite-dimensional**.\n\n- Otherwise, if there is no finite basis, the vector space is called **infinite-dimensional**.\n\nIn linear algebra, we mostly consider finite-dimensional vector spaces." :tutorial/id #uuid "e433abf5-e941-5e63-9642-c3f90b4716a7" :tutorial/math-academy-id 4226 :tutorial/title "Finite and Infinite-Dimensional Vector Spaces"} :step/id #uuid "1510c9cb-5dac-5d6c-994f-d10e8a6d47b7" :step/math-academy-id 25615 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120733}}] :activity/title "The Dimension of a Span" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186120056}] [{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046659} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186424598} :activity/id #uuid "948a33eb-6d1f-58c5-9917-674dc68468c6" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048218 :topic/id #uuid "893a2f73-ff77-57dc-a5aa-12aa27975a06" :topic/math-academy-id 1866} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186424596 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186348199 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0e3095f8-ebd6-5417-b5fa-228deb53654e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345465} {:answer/id #uuid "1ffb8a8a-2e10-5113-8365-30ccd72226d2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345633} {:answer/id #uuid "5a9aee49-f085-5882-8939-280b36b2183b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346122} {:answer/id #uuid "9125c680-3dee-5b40-9892-fbcf815bf7f0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346617} {:answer/id #uuid "f560de8e-3259-522f-ba63-0c6f8892c7a1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347524}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186346122} :answer-field/id #uuid "1ce3662f-50d2-5467-a302-1a748f1ec224" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347657}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "4dbe4152-1f13-5417-b4be-c6eaa0530f7e" :question/math-academy-id "q-79313" :question/problem "$\\text{dim}(\\text{Span}\\{\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}\\})=$" :question/worked-solution "We start by creating a matrix whose columns are made up of our vectors, and then we reduce the matrix to row echelon form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{bmatrix}4 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn this case, our matrix is already in a row echelon form.\n\nThe pivot columns correspond to basis vectors, while the non-pivot columns correspond to linearly dependent vectors that can be removed. Here, the reduced matrix has $2$ pivot columns (or alternatively, $2$ non-zero rows). \n\nTherefore,\n$\\text{dim}(\\text{Span}\\{\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}\\})=2.$"} :step/id #uuid "0af671dd-0c3a-50f4-a41a-e189faada01e" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186424600}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186424597 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186321062 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0077e2b0-9780-54fe-afa4-b670dc9fed69" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186319393} {:answer/id #uuid "3acae8da-de1f-55f4-a6ed-13701c01de6b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186319570} {:answer/id #uuid "3b0903cd-0495-574d-b41e-abbe3013ddf4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186319571} {:answer/id #uuid "87860f14-7358-57f6-a4a5-2a37b1f9c9ad" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186319798} {:answer/id #uuid "cf85f8ab-e663-568c-9644-773ac18a9dfd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186320028}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186319798} :answer-field/id #uuid "cc4b9fed-d8b4-5a97-89f5-38bb7b5b308d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186320596}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "2304859f-a364-5093-b7c5-56d02eda6877" :question/math-academy-id "q-35298" :question/problem "$\\text{dim}(\\text{Span}\\{\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -4\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ 6\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}5 \\\\ -10\\end{bmatrix}\\})=$" :question/worked-solution "We start by creating a matrix whose columns are made up of our vectors, and then we reduce the matrix to row echelon form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\begin{bmatrix}2 & -3 & 5 \\\\ -4 & 6 & -10\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+2{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -3 & 5 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe pivot columns correspond to basis vectors, while the non-pivot columns correspond to linearly dependent vectors that can be removed. Here, the reduced matrix has $1$ pivot column (or alternatively, it has exactly $1$ non-zero row). \n\nTherefore, \n$\\text{dim}(\\text{Span}\\{\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -4\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ 6\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}5 \\\\ -10\\end{bmatrix}\\})=1.$"} :step/id #uuid "166d7282-f4b0-5e00-b772-331ab9716c49" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186424596}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186424598 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186424594 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3a113c4d-a0ac-5651-906e-41d84f5d836e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186424582} {:answer/id #uuid "5368a6fd-1c3c-5ec5-98c7-f3629d82df88" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186424583} {:answer/id #uuid "78554db1-b432-51b4-936b-3b8941909583" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186424585} {:answer/id #uuid "a841feda-cb6b-5b98-990a-d1a0abf6cfb1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186424588} {:answer/id #uuid "ff9dadc2-a499-5eb3-a904-a3da6ea8a443" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186424591}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186424588} :answer-field/id #uuid "0fd465c3-4977-5d35-b197-a5e9f9656a35" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186424592}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "4780154a-e6c5-5212-9585-41f4b2c7d38a" :question/math-academy-id "q-34832" :question/problem "$\\text{dim}(\\text{Span}\\{\\begin{bmatrix}5 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}-10 \\\\ 5\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}15 \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix}\\})=$" :question/worked-solution "We start by creating a matrix whose columns are made up of our vectors, and then we reduce the matrix to row echelon form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\begin{bmatrix}5 & -10 & 15 \\\\ -1 & 5 & -3\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+\\frac{1}{5}{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}5 & -10 & 15 \\\\ 0 & 3 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe pivot columns correspond to basis vectors, while the non-pivot columns correspond to linearly dependent vectors that can be removed. Here, the reduced matrix has $2$ pivot columns (or alternatively, it has exactly $2$ non-zero rows). \n\nTherefore, \n$\\text{dim}(\\text{Span}\\{\\begin{bmatrix}5 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}-10 \\\\ 5\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}15 \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix}\\})=2.$"} :step/id #uuid "5ee4ca5d-e09c-5ab8-a217-675407324399" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186424597}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186424599 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186348205 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "85518922-a8e2-5430-be49-fc5bd9618d27" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186451723} {:answer/id #uuid "32d23fcd-416f-5d3c-bfbd-0efc26263051" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186451724}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186451724} :answer-field/id #uuid "6feab46e-0164-581d-b9ac-417f088f9a3e" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452709} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "153dcd4c-f868-5cb9-9348-c787feeb008f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186451725} {:answer/id #uuid "db92ed10-887e-5be5-8e25-a589761415a1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186451726}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186451726} :answer-field/id #uuid "7dae3873-2189-5c0f-a6cd-a4ed4645e74e" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452710} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "fb8f64e6-edb1-5d35-982e-d14dca05b3f1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186451727} {:answer/id #uuid "f8ffa827-33a1-5d4c-86a0-6a5b242d8e94" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186451728}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186451727} :answer-field/id #uuid "6f7b874c-9786-5d4f-b21e-8d93eb56af6a" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452711}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "64e956c1-f323-5e83-8c0c-e6640c910184" :question/math-academy-id "q-79326" :question/problem "Let ${v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3},{v}_{4}$ be linearly independent vectors, and suppose that the vector $w$ can be written as a linear combination of ${v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3}+{v}_{4}.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** ${v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3},{v}_{4}$ form a basis of $\\text{Span}\\{{v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3},{v}_{4}\\}$\n\n- **II.** $\\text{dim}(\\text{Span}\\{{v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3},{v}_{4}\\})=4$\n\n- **III.** $\\text{dim}(\\text{Span}\\{{v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3},{v}_{4},w\\})=5$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that a basis of a vector space $V$ is a set of linearly independent vectors that span $V.$ The dimension of $V$ is the number of vectors in any basis of $V$ (if such a basis exists). \n\nWith that in mind, let's consider each of the statements.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, $\\text{Span}\\{{v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3},{v}_{4}\\}$ is spanned by the vectors by ${v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3},{v}_{4}.$ Moreover, ${v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3},{v}_{4}$ are linearly independent vectors. So, ${v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3},{v}_{4}$ is a basis of the span.\n\n- Statement II is true. Indeed, $\\text{Span}\\{{v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3},{v}_{4}\\}$ is spanned by ${v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3},{v}_{4}$, and these are linearly independent vectors. So, ${v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3},{v}_{4}$ is a basis of the span. Therefore, we have $\\text{dim}(\\text{Span}\\{{v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3},{v}_{4}\\})=4.$\n\n- Statement III is false. We are told that $w$ can be written as a linear combination of ${v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3}+{v}_{4}.$ Hence,$w$ can be written as a linear combination of ${v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3},{v}_{4}.$ this means that $\\text{Span}\\{{v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3},{v}_{4},w\\}$ is completely spanned by ${v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3},{v}_{4}$ only. So, $\\text{dim}(\\text{Span}\\{{v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3},{v}_{4},w\\})=4$ (not $5$).\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\""} :step/id #uuid "7637b798-0ec3-5970-8fd6-abfce643c348"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186424600 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186424595 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "73c49f7c-a1f3-56e9-95d3-4e8d7959ede8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186424584} {:answer/id #uuid "93ff253e-bb05-5ca6-8757-db5007d7fe23" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186424586} {:answer/id #uuid "9b73c0f1-2d21-5d2b-981e-9083bbda05f0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186424587} {:answer/id #uuid "bc38effb-2332-5570-8872-70a5b22fb92e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186424589} {:answer/id #uuid "cbcba230-afee-51b7-a5c4-70037d514241" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186424590}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186424590} :answer-field/id #uuid "29131f28-d633-5715-a931-1a4a0aae454c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186424593}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "f427e4e2-13a5-5d7b-8b8d-103e6b378823" :question/math-academy-id "q-79320" :question/problem "Determine the dimension of $\\text{Span}\\{\\begin{bmatrix}\\sqrt{2} \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix}\\}.$" :question/worked-solution "We start by creating a matrix whose columns are made up of our vectors, and then we reduce the matrix to row echelon form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{bmatrix}\\sqrt{2} & 1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -3\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn this case, our matrix is already in a row echelon form.\n\nThe pivot columns correspond to basis vectors, while the non-pivot columns correspond to linearly dependent vectors that can be removed. Here, the reduced matrix has $3$ pivot columns (or alternatively, $3$ non-zero rows). \n\nTherefore, the dimension of the span is $3.$"} :step/id #uuid "8b0be1b0-7fd8-51b2-a3b6-8c5d972e2e97" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186424599}}] :activity/title "The Dimension of a Span" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186424581}] [{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046659} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186481713} :activity/id #uuid "51d818d1-6343-54db-9ec7-6770312306d5" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048218 :topic/id #uuid "893a2f73-ff77-57dc-a5aa-12aa27975a06" :topic/math-academy-id 1866} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186481679 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186321065 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "267fce2f-8f79-53e9-831e-7c2a90e2adc3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "16" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186319510} {:answer/id #uuid "345eb41a-6222-5988-930d-6af162d31248" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186319552} {:answer/id #uuid "48471577-d3ba-5647-a23c-9be647a9f57c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186319607} {:answer/id #uuid "96266788-11c1-5a60-bc6e-f7ab6b25cc84" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186319834} {:answer/id #uuid "a1b8e6ac-3f8b-59ff-9fcc-f59861669f49" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186319873}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186319607} :answer-field/id #uuid "eca04464-a958-5b61-98f7-1d33e413d898" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186320654}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "2a41f8fd-4b7f-554c-b544-a6304735d193" :question/math-academy-id "q-35302" :question/problem "$\\text{dim}(\\text{Span}\\{\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}5 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}-6 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}\\})=$" :question/worked-solution "We start by creating a matrix whose columns are made up of our vectors, and then we reduce the matrix to row echelon form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 5 & -6 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -2 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 0 & 2 & -1 \\\\ 0 & -2 & 4 & -2\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+(-\\frac{1}{2}){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 5 & -6 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -\\frac{5}{2} & 5 & -\\frac{5}{2} \\\\ 0 & -2 & 4 & -2\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+\\frac{5}{2}{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 5 & -6 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -2 & 4 & -2\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{4} & :={R}_{4}+2{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 5 & -6 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe pivot columns correspond to basis vectors, while the non-pivot columns correspond to linearly dependent vectors that can be removed. Here, the reduced matrix has $2$ pivot columns (or alternatively, it has exactly $2$ non-zero rows). \n\nTherefore, \n$\\text{dim}(\\text{Span}\\{\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}5 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}-6 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}\\})=2.$"} :step/id #uuid "198f6a30-b5d2-5121-8dad-46d3eabe550e" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186481745}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186481707 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186481376 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "04f4d151-1cd0-5621-ba89-6470f4af573e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186480746} {:answer/id #uuid "86ae4f21-6981-5f72-90e2-a3f32de9f3c2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "15" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186481000} {:answer/id #uuid "8fca35bb-c3b8-5e45-a1f8-dfab2c842147" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186481008} {:answer/id #uuid "9c56e737-b927-5ce7-a48f-7ae0b922a01d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186481039} {:answer/id #uuid "bb990b9f-4a58-59bd-858f-c85000fc92e2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186481093}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186481039} :answer-field/id #uuid "010bf50f-8de4-58ce-a0a7-3ef04178a4cc" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186481238}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "656e0655-2fab-5e41-bca0-28f4bd148f34" :question/math-academy-id "q-35293" :question/problem "$\\text{dim}(\\text{Span}\\{\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 6\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ 6 \\\\ 9\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}5 \\\\ -10 \\\\ -15\\end{bmatrix}\\})=$" :question/worked-solution "We start by creating a matrix whose columns are made up of our vectors, and then we reduce the matrix to row echelon form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\begin{bmatrix}-2 & -3 & 5 \\\\ 4 & 6 & -10 \\\\ 6 & 9 & -15\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+2{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & -3 & 5 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 6 & 9 & -15\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+3{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & -3 & 5 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe pivot columns correspond to basis vectors, while the non-pivot columns correspond to linearly dependent vectors that can be removed. Here, the reduced matrix has $1$ pivot column (or alternatively, it has exactly $1$ non-zero row). \n\nTherefore, \n$\\text{dim}(\\text{Span}\\{\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 4 \\\\ -6\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ 6 \\\\ 9\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}5 \\\\ -10 \\\\ -15\\end{bmatrix}\\})=1.$"} :step/id #uuid "6693ff7f-3150-52b2-a5bf-c9e333e1dfa0" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186481679}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186481713 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186321058 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "85d268a4-da68-5461-a5fd-4325487f2486" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186319793} {:answer/id #uuid "8e081505-3e87-527d-928a-050077ac043e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186319815} {:answer/id #uuid "a9f2b1f8-3c4d-5787-adea-b1ec3788961d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186319903} {:answer/id #uuid "dca55a5a-8830-5a03-b9ee-73e0f828dbe2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186320062} {:answer/id #uuid "fb52be77-be13-5cd3-9f52-a1bc052f07d4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186320148}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186320148} :answer-field/id #uuid "e593bb99-2134-5208-8b24-97c01ed0e7f1" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186320638}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "8505e165-2993-5e50-9039-0c1c0e794caf" :question/math-academy-id "q-35292" :question/problem "Determine the dimension of\n$\\text{Span}\\{\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -5 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}-7 \\\\ -9 \\\\ 6\\end{bmatrix}\\}.$" :question/worked-solution "We start by creating a matrix whose columns are made up of our vectors, and then we reduce the matrix to row echelon form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\begin{bmatrix}1 & 4 & -7 \\\\ -5 & 2 & -9 \\\\ 0 & -3 & 6\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+5{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 4 & -7 \\\\ 0 & 22 & -44 \\\\ 0 & -3 & 6\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+\\frac{3}{22}{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 4 & -7 \\\\ 0 & 22 & -44 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe pivot columns correspond to basis vectors, while the non-pivot columns correspond to linearly dependent vectors that can be removed. Here, the reduced matrix has $2$ pivot columns (or alternatively, it has exactly $2$ non-zero rows). \n\nTherefore, the dimension of the span is $2.$"} :step/id #uuid "79a898d8-d586-5a50-942e-6819f484480b" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186481707}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186481714 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186348204 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4dcdf7ad-dc8c-5b97-9b5e-c7b134b49b20" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186451717} {:answer/id #uuid "6c5f343f-9134-5395-8a95-5714eed53c21" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186451718}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186451718} :answer-field/id #uuid "0c5ea2e4-dd11-528a-87f8-7c77094a84a7" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452706} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "996635a3-7de7-52e1-9885-d13e9600bdb0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186451719} {:answer/id #uuid "bf9a86f7-257f-52de-b0bc-b84e386c256a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186451720}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186451720} :answer-field/id #uuid "90409394-7c69-5b92-8b05-88400b81ffd6" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452707} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6ed5a0f9-b208-53b1-948d-e048fe02a61a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186451721} {:answer/id #uuid "f080355d-0cd5-5f51-97e4-a3f5a882e7c0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186451722}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186451722} :answer-field/id #uuid "f5f3fcac-5294-5f8b-8c36-f97bba56f78e" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452708}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "1fa1e936-cb5b-5073-aaa6-f5f5e931fac3" :question/math-academy-id "q-79323" :question/problem "Let ${v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3}$ be linearly independent vectors, and suppose that the vector $w$ can be written as a linear combination of ${v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3}.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** ${v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3}$ form a basis of $\\text{Span}{{v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3},w}$\n- **II.** $\\text{dim}(\\text{Span}{{v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3}})=3$\n- **III.** $\\text{dim}(\\text{Span}{{v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3},w})=3$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that a basis of a vector space $V$ is a set of linearly independent vectors that span $V.$ The dimension of $V$ is the number of vectors in any basis of $V$ (if such a basis exists). \n\nWith that in mind, let's consider each of the statements.\n\n- Statement I is true. We are told that $w$ can be written as a linear combination of ${v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3}.$ This means that $\\text{Span}{{v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3},w}$ is completely spanned by the vectors ${v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3}$ only, and we are told that ${v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3}$ are linearly independent vectors. So, ${v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3}$ is a basis of the span.\n\n- Statement II is true. Indeed, $\\text{Span}{{v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3}}$ is spanned by ${v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3}$, and these are linearly independent vectors. So, ${v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3}$ is a basis of the span.Therefore, we have $\\text{dim}(\\text{Span}{{v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3}})=3.$\n\n- Statement III is true. Since ${v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3}$ is a basis of the given span and this basis contains $3$ vectors, we have $\\text{dim}(\\text{Span}{{v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3},w})=3.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III.\""} :step/id #uuid "7b1a2415-933b-51b1-9386-7c5884723ec9"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186481745 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120530 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d9d9b911-3c3f-58c5-b3b6-d44535560355" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186119295} {:answer/id #uuid "df4d4fcd-8faf-530e-b415-5c1f3fed2066" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186119296} {:answer/id #uuid "e52dbb26-c23e-57e3-b4cf-35ee9f0f3f51" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186119297} {:answer/id #uuid "0de4b9c7-15f2-56b8-8c4e-d86894ba45f5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186119298} {:answer/id #uuid "4bb91a0b-9c2a-5f24-90dc-175b0eb4a659" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186119299}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119295} :answer-field/id #uuid "0ac61baf-9210-59b4-8248-21725e9864ee" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186119294}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "5b165675-1f3d-55a7-a1a0-5f0b3adccb41" :question/math-academy-id "q-79149" :question/problem "Let ${v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3}$ be linearly independent vectors, and suppose that the vector $w$ can be written as a linear combination of ${v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3}.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. ${v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3}$ form a basis of $\\text{Span}\\{{v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3}\\}$\n\nII. $\\text{dim}(\\text{Span}\\{{v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3}\\})=3$\n\nIII. $\\text{dim}(\\text{Span}\\{{v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3},w\\})=4$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that a basis of a vector space $V$ is a set of linearly independent vectors that span $V.$ The dimension of $V$ is the number of vectors in any basis of $V$ (if such a basis exists). \n\nWith that in mind, let's consider each of the statements.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, $\\text{Span}\\{{v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3}\\}$ is spanned by ${v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3}$, and we are told that ${v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3}$ are linearly independent vectors. So, ${v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3}$ is a basis of the span.\n\n- Statement II is true. Since ${v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3}$ is a basis of the given span and this basis contains $3$ vectors, we have $\\text{dim}(\\text{Span}\\{{v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3}\\})=3.$\n\n- Statement III is false. We are told that $w$ can be written as a linear combination of ${v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3}.$ This means that $\\text{Span}\\{{v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3},w\\}$ is completely spanned by the vectors ${v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3}$ only. So, $\\text{dim}(\\text{Span}\\{{v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3},w\\})=3$ (not $4$).\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\""} :step/id #uuid "f48062a4-16f1-54ef-8816-9c650541ca65" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186481714}}] :activity/title "The Dimension of a Span" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186481571}] [{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046658} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186483496} :activity/id #uuid "7ba47f17-0fae-5ac4-aeae-fb08378b5bc9" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048218 :topic/id #uuid "893a2f73-ff77-57dc-a5aa-12aa27975a06" :topic/math-academy-id 1866} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186483440 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186348199 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0e3095f8-ebd6-5417-b5fa-228deb53654e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345465} {:answer/id #uuid "1ffb8a8a-2e10-5113-8365-30ccd72226d2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345633} {:answer/id #uuid "5a9aee49-f085-5882-8939-280b36b2183b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346122} {:answer/id #uuid "9125c680-3dee-5b40-9892-fbcf815bf7f0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346617} {:answer/id #uuid "f560de8e-3259-522f-ba63-0c6f8892c7a1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347524}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186346122} :answer-field/id #uuid "1ce3662f-50d2-5467-a302-1a748f1ec224" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347657}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "4dbe4152-1f13-5417-b4be-c6eaa0530f7e" :question/math-academy-id "q-79313" :question/problem "$\\text{dim}(\\text{Span}\\{\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}\\})=$" :question/worked-solution "We start by creating a matrix whose columns are made up of our vectors, and then we reduce the matrix to row echelon form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{bmatrix}4 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn this case, our matrix is already in a row echelon form.\n\nThe pivot columns correspond to basis vectors, while the non-pivot columns correspond to linearly dependent vectors that can be removed. Here, the reduced matrix has $2$ pivot columns (or alternatively, $2$ non-zero rows). \n\nTherefore,\n$\\text{dim}(\\text{Span}\\{\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}\\})=2.$"} :step/id #uuid "17a473c6-6c8d-5968-aa6a-8059d32a39f1" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186483463}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186483452 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278261 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "816648e9-966e-5cf2-839c-30024fe1baea" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186449844} {:answer/id #uuid "5555ba44-b062-5e8b-b183-16129f0fc2a1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186449845}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186449845} :answer-field/id #uuid "97a0fd59-a9ee-584e-8be4-712573413f2c" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186450876} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a963f06c-c986-5d91-9ccc-f663b195f77d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186449846} {:answer/id #uuid "f9054fa5-b0f9-5ece-93e7-1ee2ccb1796b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186449847}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186449846} :answer-field/id #uuid "c53ef9e0-ce91-5531-b56d-d801d314355e" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186450877} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f0872989-936e-53ad-80f1-db5035c605ee" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186449848} {:answer/id #uuid "355ac780-1b2e-598e-a421-8ddc9710eaf7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186449849}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186449848} :answer-field/id #uuid "adc6cc33-91be-5597-916f-94e4653b4dba" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186450878}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "2ffee0ea-a323-5b51-850e-98d4c4b547d9" :question/math-academy-id "q-159784" :question/problem "Let ${v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3}$ be linearly independent vectors, and suppose that the vector $w$ can be written as a linear combination of ${v}_{1}$ and ${v}_{2}.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** ${v}_{1},{v}_{2}$ form a basis of $\\text{Span}{{v}_{1},{v}_{2},w}$\n- **II.** $\\text{dim}(\\text{Span}{{v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3},w})=4$\n- **III.** $\\text{dim}(\\text{Span}{{v}_{1},{v}_{2}})=3$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that a basis of a vector space $V$ is a set of linearly independent vectors that span $V.$ The dimension of $V$ is the number of vectors in any basis of $V$ (if such a basis exists). \n\nWith that in mind, let's consider each of the statements.\n\n- Statement I is true. We are told that $w$ can be written as a linear combination of ${v}_{1},{v}_{2}.$ This means that $\\text{Span}{{v}_{1},{v}_{2},w}$ is completely spanned by the vectors ${v}_{1},{v}_{2}$ only. So, ${v}_{1},{v}_{2}$ form a basis of $\\text{Span}{{v}_{1},{v}_{2},w}$\n\n- Statement II is false. We are told that $w$ can be written as a linear combination of ${v}_{1},{v}_{2},$ and we are told that ${v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3}$ are linearly independent vectors. This means that $\\text{Span}{{v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3},w}$ is completely spanned by the vectors ${v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3}$ only. So, ${v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3}$ is a basis of the span. Therefore, we have $\\text{dim}(\\text{Span}{{v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3},w})=3$ (not $4$).\n\n- Statement III is false. Indeed, $\\text{Span}{{v}_{1},{v}_{2}}$ is spanned by ${v}_{1},{v}_{2}$, and we are told that ${v}_{1},{v}_{2}$ are linearly independent vectors. So, ${v}_{1},{v}_{2}$ is a basis of the span. Therefore, we have $\\text{dim}(\\text{Span}{{v}_{1},{v}_{2}})=2$ (not $3$).\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\""} :step/id #uuid "34dded57-bc20-5c74-8a7c-26db7d4b2c49"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186483463 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186483398 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2674be6f-e836-53b6-ad75-f49bcd215297" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186482879} {:answer/id #uuid "2fdaf3df-3466-5a46-9d91-de545d17a174" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186482896} {:answer/id #uuid "628e0240-3161-5a31-bec3-d0d78a344f09" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186482984} {:answer/id #uuid "ca9bbb01-90f1-5914-b05b-4fdbb5c155e7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186483149} {:answer/id #uuid "d68f1746-c721-55d0-abfa-7820321d3544" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186483176}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186482879} :answer-field/id #uuid "6d524d4c-bdb9-5865-a9c3-811956ada022" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186483279}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "b149c895-74ab-53c4-8e34-ba4f249fb566" :question/math-academy-id "q-79314" :question/problem "$\\text{dim}(\\text{Span}\\{\\begin{bmatrix}-5 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}\\})=$" :question/worked-solution "We start by creating a matrix whose columns are made up of our vectors, and then we reduce the matrix to row echelon form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{bmatrix}-5 & 2 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn this case, our matrix is already in a row echelon form.\n\nThe pivot columns correspond to basis vectors, while the non-pivot columns correspond to linearly dependent vectors that can be removed. Here, the reduced matrix has $2$ pivot columns (or alternatively, $2$ non-zero rows). \n\nTherefore,\n$\\text{dim}(\\text{Span}\\{\\begin{bmatrix}-5 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}\\})=2.$"} :step/id #uuid "60bb4227-9814-5e8d-9517-43a71cd69291" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186483452}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186483495 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186481376 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "04f4d151-1cd0-5621-ba89-6470f4af573e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186480746} {:answer/id #uuid "86ae4f21-6981-5f72-90e2-a3f32de9f3c2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "15" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186481000} {:answer/id #uuid "8fca35bb-c3b8-5e45-a1f8-dfab2c842147" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186481008} {:answer/id #uuid "9c56e737-b927-5ce7-a48f-7ae0b922a01d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186481039} {:answer/id #uuid "bb990b9f-4a58-59bd-858f-c85000fc92e2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186481093}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186481039} :answer-field/id #uuid "010bf50f-8de4-58ce-a0a7-3ef04178a4cc" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186481238}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "656e0655-2fab-5e41-bca0-28f4bd148f34" :question/math-academy-id "q-35293" :question/problem "$\\text{dim}(\\text{Span}\\{\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 6\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ 6 \\\\ 9\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}5 \\\\ -10 \\\\ -15\\end{bmatrix}\\})=$" :question/worked-solution "We start by creating a matrix whose columns are made up of our vectors, and then we reduce the matrix to row echelon form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\begin{bmatrix}-2 & -3 & 5 \\\\ 4 & 6 & -10 \\\\ 6 & 9 & -15\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+2{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & -3 & 5 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 6 & 9 & -15\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+3{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & -3 & 5 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe pivot columns correspond to basis vectors, while the non-pivot columns correspond to linearly dependent vectors that can be removed. Here, the reduced matrix has $1$ pivot column (or alternatively, it has exactly $1$ non-zero row). \n\nTherefore, \n$\\text{dim}(\\text{Span}\\{\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 4 \\\\ -6\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ 6 \\\\ 9\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}5 \\\\ -10 \\\\ -15\\end{bmatrix}\\})=1.$"} :step/id #uuid "c8698e91-362f-52ce-8632-aab433d3ae0e" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186483440}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186483496 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120365 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "841b51ab-4624-5135-9e62-a5d16f7f40d2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186118305} {:answer/id #uuid "8c5c3ff9-d114-5657-833d-571463f53ae1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186118306} {:answer/id #uuid "0095d96f-0e33-50d5-bec2-0593e0bb2a44" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186118307} {:answer/id #uuid "0bd230b8-acb7-514d-8c02-14cbb3854aa5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186118308} {:answer/id #uuid "43fd4f00-8337-5638-b2ff-f732db2e10d3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186118309}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186118308} :answer-field/id #uuid "cbe09fbe-4ea8-5884-b7ed-064efcb293bf" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186118304}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "c3ea0ae2-1531-5d26-b98b-bdd05134f6b8" :question/math-academy-id "q-34811" :question/problem "Determine the dimension of $\\text{Span}\\{\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}\\}.$" :question/worked-solution "We start by creating a matrix whose columns are made up of our vectors, and then we reduce the matrix to row echelon form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\begin{bmatrix}2 & -2 & 2 \\\\ -2 & 1 & -2 \\\\ 1 & -1 & 3\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -2 & 2 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & -1 & 3\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+(-\\frac{1}{2}){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -2 & 2 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 2\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe pivot columns correspond to basis vectors, while the non-pivot columns correspond to linearly dependent vectors that can be removed. Here, the reduced matrix has $3$ pivot columns (or alternatively, it has exactly $3$ non-zero rows). \n\nTherefore, the dimension of the span is $3.$"} :step/id #uuid "ca098e19-bec0-502e-83bc-ca18e83625bc" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186483495}}] :activity/title "The Dimension of a Span" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186482808}] [{:activity/expected-seconds 660.0 :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176723} :activity/id #uuid "55f43435-8dca-5810-9753-eee340bb8532" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048485 :topic/id #uuid "a385b92b-792f-5dd9-9921-9b85efbacd4d" :topic/math-academy-id 3603} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176723 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176991 :tutorial/content "Sometimes, we might know the general form of the probability distribution for a population, yet we may not know some of its underlying parameters. We wish to develop strategies to estimate unknown population parameters from sample data. One such strategy is to use **likelihood functions.** \n\nIn this lesson, we'll learn how to compute likelihood functions for particular discrete probability distributions.\n\nSuppose we have an I.I.D. (independent and identically distributed) random sample\n\n$$\nX_1, \\: X_2, \\: \\ldots, \\: X_n,\n$$\n\nwhere the probability mass function of each $X_i$ is given by\n\n$$\nf(x;\\,\\theta)\n$$\n\nand $\\theta$ is an *unknown,* fixed parameter. \n\nSuppose we have a particular sample from this distribution:\n\n$$\nx_1, \\: x_2, \\: \\ldots, \\: x_n\n$$\n\nWe define the **likelihood function** $L(\\theta)$ as \n\n$$\nL(\\theta) = P(X_1=x_1, X_2=x_2, \\ldots , X_n = x_n).\n$$\n\nThe likelihood function gives the probability of getting a particular sample for a specific parameter value. \n\nNow, since we assumed that all $X_i$'s are independent and identically distributed, by the multiplication law, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L(\\theta ) & =P({X}_{1}={x}_{1},{X}_{2}={x}_{2},…,{X}_{n}={x}_{n}) \\\\  & =P({X}_{1}={x}_{1})\\cdot P({X}_{2}={x}_{2})⋯P({X}_{n}={x}_{n}) \\\\  & ={\\prod }_{i=1}^{n}P({X}_{i}={x}_{i}) \\\\  & ={\\prod }_{i=1}^{n}f({x}_{i};\\,\\theta ).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo summarize, the likelihood function $L(\\theta)$ is defined as\n\n$$\nL(\\theta) = \\prod_{i=1}^n f(x_i;\\,\\theta).\n$$\n\nLet's take a look at a concrete example." :tutorial/id #uuid "febfe0fc-b4fd-5ec4-9fe2-66459c06433b" :tutorial/math-academy-id 5364 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "05cbc6df-2e50-58cc-b377-41b56e25e5ed" :step/math-academy-id 33494 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176882}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176845 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176040 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186175687 :question/id #uuid "d15d6003-a4dd-599f-987d-289508968ac7" :question/math-academy-id "e-10105"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "39593326-76e2-578f-af68-57f59712c30e" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048922} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049273} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049367} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049729} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186051082}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176370} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176392} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186435595} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186435597}] :knowledge-point/title "Likelihood Functions for Bernoulli Random Variables"} :step/id #uuid "819b8cb4-51d8-5f27-b9c1-7715977c4527" :step/math-academy-id 38120 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176880}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176880 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176062 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186175708 :question/id #uuid "3e3ca456-a46a-5a5b-9cf6-07caad631ff9" :question/math-academy-id "e-11117"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "2a2eb2ca-a19a-591e-810a-c028a17be252" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050651}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176371} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176393} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186435594} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186435598}] :knowledge-point/title "Likelihood Functions for Binomial Random Variables"} :step/id #uuid "808203c4-7f89-5eca-af00-29965de75553" :step/math-academy-id 42270 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176881}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176881 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176063 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186175709 :question/id #uuid "9f08cf84-3e3b-5c86-adb6-622bc51a15f6" :question/math-academy-id "e-11118"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "03d91774-9a9f-55a8-8a9a-090788318a98" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049358}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176372} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176394} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186410435} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186435596}] :knowledge-point/title "Likelihood Functions for Poisson Random Variables"} :step/id #uuid "44901d9a-fe9f-55ba-bd1c-9441ea7f0e71" :step/math-academy-id 42271} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176882 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186177074 :tutorial/content "Suppose that \n\n$$\nX_1,\\quad X_2,\\quad X_3,\\quad X_4,\\quad X_5,\\quad X_6\n$$\n\n \n\nis an I.I.D random sample from a population, where \n\n$$\nX_i\\sim \\text{Bernoulli}(\\theta)\n$$\n\nand $\\theta$ is the *unknown* probability of success. \n\nSuppose we conduct a sample and get the following data:\n\n$$\nx_1=0, \\quad x_2=1, \\quad x_3=1, \\quad x_4=0, \\quad x_5=1, \\quad x_6=1\n$$\n\n \n\nLet's compute the likelihood function for this sample.\n\nRecall that if $X_1, X_2, \\ldots, X_{n}$ are I.I.D random variables with probability mass function $f(x; \\theta),$ then the likelihood function of a random sample $x_1, x_2, \\ldots, x_n$ is given by\n\n$$\nL(\\theta) = P(X_1=x_1, X_2=x_2, \\ldots , X_n = x_n) = \\displaystyle\\prod_{i=1}^n f(x_i;\\,\\theta).\n$$\n\nThe probability mass function for a Bernoulli random variable with an unknown probability of success $\\theta$ is given by\n\n$$\nf(x; \\theta) = \\theta^x(1-\\theta)^{1-x}, \\qquad x=0,1.\n$$\n\nUsing this definition, we can calculate the likelihood function as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L(\\theta ) & ={\\prod }_{i=1}^{n}f({x}_{i};\\,\\theta ) \\\\  & ={\\prod }_{i=1}^{n}{\\theta }^{{x}_{i}}(1-\\theta )^{1-{x}_{i}} \\\\  & =({\\theta }^{{x}_{1}}(1-\\theta )^{1-{x}_{1}})\\cdot ({\\theta }^{{x}_{2}}(1-\\theta )^{1-{x}_{2}})\\,⋯\\,({\\theta }^{{x}_{n}}(1-\\theta )^{1-{x}_{n}}) \\\\  & ={\\theta }^{\\,\\sum {x}_{i}}(1-\\theta )^{\\,\\sum (1-{x}_{i})} \\\\  & ={\\theta }^{\\,\\sum {x}_{i}}(1-\\theta )^{\\,n-\\sum {x}_{i}} \\\\  & ={\\theta }^{S}(1-\\theta )^{n-S}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $\\displaystyle S = \\sum\\limits_ {i=1}^n x_i.$ \n\nFor our sample, we have that $n=6,$ and \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i} \\\\  & =0+1+1+0+1+1 \\\\  & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the likelihood function for this sample is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L(\\theta ) & ={\\theta }^{4}(1-\\theta )^{6-4} \\\\  & ={\\theta }^{4}(1-\\theta )^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe graph of $L(\\theta)$ is shown below. \n\n![](images/28/28c73749a566d5531ef2f13c5b01bd1d26ced28e19749abf056841492fb497f5.png)\n\nNotice that $L(\\theta)$ has a maximum inside the interval $\\theta \\in [0,1].$ The value $\\widehat{\\,\\theta\\,}$ that corresponds to the maximum likelihood is called the **maximum likelihood estimate** of the parameter $\\theta$ for the given sample. \n\nWe'll discuss how to compute the maximum likelihood estimate in future topics. But, for now, let's see more examples of finding the likelihood functions for different distributions and samples." :tutorial/id #uuid "8748c53f-183a-5ba8-99aa-e0a92e7d9707" :tutorial/math-academy-id 7013 :tutorial/title "A Worked Example"} :step/id #uuid "7abb8790-00de-50e4-8248-7dcfdef20be2" :step/math-academy-id 42315 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176845}}] :activity/title "Likelihood Functions for Discrete Probability Distributions" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186176143}] [{:activity/expected-seconds 420.0 :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167679} :activity/id #uuid "631d41d0-caaf-5a9a-8598-3ad89711698e" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048884 :topic/id #uuid "c96d046b-16b8-5aad-81a5-2dd906b25702" :topic/math-academy-id 3269} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167679 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186168077 :tutorial/content "Recall that a discrete uniform distribution is a probability distribution that models the outcome of a single event when the support $S$ is finite, and each $x\\in S$ is equally likely.\n\nIn general, if $X$ follows a discrete uniform distribution over a set $S,$ then $X$ has the following probability mass function:\n\n![](images/d0/d09ac993190a8f4a98e2703e8a0caa2decfbc193486bdf0352d4210e4738eb66.svg)\nwhere $|S|$ is the size (cardinality) of $S$.\n\nDiscrete uniform distributions occur regularly in games of chance. \n\nFor example, suppose that in a particular lottery, players must select a number from the following set: \n\n$$\n\\{1,2,\\ldots,50\\}\n$$\n\nLet's assume that a player randomly selects a number from this set such that each number has an equal probability of being selected. Then, if $X$ represents the number the player chooses, we can model this situation using a discrete uniform distribution, where\n\n$$\nX \\sim U \\{1,2,\\ldots,50\\}.\n$$\n\n \n\nFurther, since $S = \\{1,2,\\ldots,50\\}$ has $|S| = 50$ items, the probability mass function for the random variable $X$ is\n\n![](images/4a/4a4a14ac1547b6b995d77c579c46ce63048a9285166059708ce39c8739fdbb7d.svg)" :tutorial/id #uuid "c576c848-4a58-54d4-a011-7e6c489c8225" :tutorial/math-academy-id 5101 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "79d64508-f3b9-51ba-b040-3e3b55ece653" :step/math-academy-id 32013 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167769}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167680 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166838 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166680 :question/id #uuid "dad3d0a2-26e0-52c4-9e5a-abe6664cc02c" :question/math-academy-id "e-8616"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "e0e2ce6a-ee66-50a0-88b4-47dc0189ff51" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167504} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167514} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186424374} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186424376} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186424378} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186424379} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186476943}] :knowledge-point/title "Modeling a Situation in Context With the Discrete Uniform Distribution"} :step/id #uuid "1cf23694-2167-55c4-9d0e-c4724313b90c" :step/math-academy-id 32014} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167684 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166841 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166687 :question/id #uuid "de4ae41e-c981-5b5e-b52c-c8e7caf4c272" :question/math-academy-id "e-8647"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "00b14560-29f9-58fc-ac65-5505987579fb" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048404}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167513} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167516} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186424375} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186424377} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186424380} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186476918} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186476936} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186476976}] :knowledge-point/title "Determining Situations That Can Be Modeled as a Discrete Uniform Distribution"} :step/id #uuid "832deb31-bd88-5bbe-acc1-91719627f9fd" :step/math-academy-id 32093 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167680}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167769 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186168134 :tutorial/content "To apply a discrete uniform probability model to a situation in context, there are two things we need to check for:\n\n- The support $S$ of our random variable should be *finite*, and\n\n- each $x\\in S$ must be *equally likely*.\n\nThere are some common pitfalls that we need to be aware of!\n\n- First, we cannot use a discrete uniform distribution to model continuous phenomena.\n\nFor example, weight, time, and distance are all quantities that can take *any* real number within a given interval. Such quantities should be modeled as continuous, so a discrete uniform distribution is usually inappropriate.\n\n- Second, we must be wary of discrete phenomena requiring infinitely large support.\n\nFor example, suppose $X$ represents the number of road accidents at a particular junction in a given month. Now, while $X$ is (hopefully) a very small number, it could theoretically be *any* positive integer for modeling purposes! For this reason, a variable with infinite support is more appropriate. Moreover, $X$ is unlikely to be uniformly distributed since having $X=2$ accidents per month is more likely than $X=1\\,000\\,000.$" :tutorial/id #uuid "aabc2db5-2a28-567d-94ed-1b215e353ccc" :tutorial/math-academy-id 6457 :tutorial/title "Determining When a Discrete Uniform Distribution is Appropriate"} :step/id #uuid "1abe1dd3-86a6-56dd-9bbf-f7a1402c711c" :step/math-academy-id 39012 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167684}}] :activity/title "Modeling With Discrete Uniform Distributions" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186167033}] [{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046658} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186476978} :activity/id #uuid "b52aa1a3-043f-5121-8a08-3b2a8fa0397f" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048884 :topic/id #uuid "c96d046b-16b8-5aad-81a5-2dd906b25702" :topic/math-academy-id 3269} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186476978 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167504 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "87432d69-96bb-52d4-838d-ce1f6c01f820" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186166233} {:answer/id #uuid "f17e4e3f-84c4-5252-b652-12737eea7c7d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186166234} {:answer/id #uuid "ff361c42-9dab-56e8-a7fa-2e6229a27e33" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186166235} {:answer/id #uuid "1e8d4f72-411d-5d3f-88dd-11650e216ee8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{6}{11}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186166236} {:answer/id #uuid "59e50954-5813-5a9b-9c99-a4c994779e1a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{11}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186166237}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166237} :answer-field/id #uuid "c48a4e74-626d-5c6c-a256-eb8d5fc6d57b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186166232}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "10558913-f96c-5656-8d0d-6962114123ec" :question/math-academy-id "q-97231" :question/problem "In a game show, a contestant can choose a prize from any box. The boxes are labeled with numbers $5$ through $15.$ If the contestant chooses a box at random, what is the probability that the box number they pick is less than $10?$" :question/worked-solution "Let $X$ represent the number on the chosen box. From the problem statement, we know that $X∼U\\{5,6,…,15\\},$ and we wish to compute $P(X<10).$\n\nIn general, if $X$ follows a discrete uniform distribution over a set $S,$ then $X$ has the following probability mass function:\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{|S|}, & x\\in S \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHere, we have $X∼U\\{5,6,…,15\\},$ and the set $S=\\{5,6,…,15\\}$ has $|S|=15-5+1=11$ items. So, we have\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{11}, & x=5,6,…,15 \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn $S,$ the items that satisfy $X<10$ are $5,6,7,8,9.$ There are $5$ of these items. Therefore,\n\n$$\nP(X<10)=5\\cdot \\frac{1}{11}=\\frac{5}{11}.\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "0afaf5eb-a1a3-531d-950b-531438c2a1c3" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186476983}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186476983 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186476943 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9113dee1-1184-5d4f-aa27-da463ed39bea" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{7}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186476514} {:answer/id #uuid "a0d64684-dede-539a-83c4-b43a77b362ba" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186476554} {:answer/id #uuid "af717fad-e100-59bb-b086-82c6e11d1bb0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186476594} {:answer/id #uuid "b762868b-9280-5c3f-8b90-87d5f4955e86" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186476618} {:answer/id #uuid "ca885fbf-1c70-5e4e-a280-d699b127bab2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{7}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186476657}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186476554} :answer-field/id #uuid "a68816de-9c55-5f5d-8b88-343571543dfb" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186476832}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "aef0f15c-e4b9-546e-bc50-4ed011233821" :question/math-academy-id "q-97675" :question/problem "A particular traditional festival is usually held on a single day after the $15$th and before the $26$th of August. The exact day is selected each year and depends on the weather conditions. Each day after the $15$th and before the $26$th of August has an equal probability of being chosen. What is the probability that the festival will take place before the $18$th of August next year?" :question/worked-solution "Let $X$ represent the date of the festival. From the problem statement, we know that $X∼U\\{16,17,…,25\\},$ and we wish to compute $P(X<18).$\n\nIn general, if $X$ follows a discrete uniform distribution over a set $S,$ then $X$ has the following probability mass function:\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{|S|}, & x\\in S \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHere, we have $X∼U\\{16,17,…,25\\},$ and the set $S=\\{16,17,…,25\\}$ has $|S|=25-16+1=10$ items. So, we have\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{10}, & x=16,17,…,25 \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn $S,$ the items that satisfy $X<18$ are $16,17.$ There are $2$ of these elements. Therefore,\n\n$$\nP(X<18)=2\\cdot \\frac{1}{10}=\\frac{1}{5}.\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "3b576ed1-e681-535b-add8-5a4e3ec59f62" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186476997}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186476992 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186476918 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "18be3bb8-ad9a-59a0-8e96-a2e78ebe90d2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186476246} {:answer/id #uuid "3e724cf9-e700-5717-b132-e0b59222dcbe" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186476340} {:answer/id #uuid "556001da-9106-5edd-8a11-feee34eef773" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186476383} {:answer/id #uuid "95680a68-d4fb-5598-89ee-cb697902cdbf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186476523} {:answer/id #uuid "b9aeb474-76b7-58b7-8e98-f5f416fc7fb9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186476628}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186476628} :answer-field/id #uuid "db741232-8680-5d6a-bb33-fb3ba535c7c2" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186476854}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "7feb9bd7-7758-5cb1-b94d-d4d0a23f7141" :question/math-academy-id "q-148275" :question/problem "Which of the following random variables can be modeled as a discrete uniform distribution?\n\nI. The number of visitors to a museum in an hour.\n\nII. The number obtained when rolling a fair die.\n\nIII. The amount of time, in minutes, between successive full charges in a smartphone." :question/worked-solution "We can use the discrete uniform distribution to model the outcome of a single event when the support $S$ is finite, and each $x\\in S$ is equally likely.\n\nWith that in mind, let's consider each of the given random variables.\n\n- Random variable I cannot be modeled as a discrete uniform distribution. This is because the number of visitors can be any positive integer. Moreover, each number of visitors is not equally likely.\n\n- Random variable II can be modeled as a discrete uniform distribution. This is because there are a finite number of integers as possible outcomes, between $1$ and $6,$ and since the die is fair, each integer has an equal probability of being selected.\n\n- Random variable III cannot be modeled as a discrete uniform distribution. This is because the amount of time between events is a real number, so the support is not finite.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\""} :step/id #uuid "9110cd90-d856-52de-b2a7-c1f02c3ad17c"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186476997 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186476976 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2002af14-f31e-5774-95b4-7d9d82ee7285" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186476275} {:answer/id #uuid "207fa16c-fd2e-53b6-a610-778aebbf45b6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "II and III ony" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186476278} {:answer/id #uuid "3481be1a-af70-5da3-9fd8-e272a09d8a44" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186476320} {:answer/id #uuid "71900d00-b9e3-581f-9e4c-75f17d668885" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "None" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186476459} {:answer/id #uuid "75fcae69-fdbf-5035-96b4-24a5fcae7c11" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186476467}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186476459} :answer-field/id #uuid "23db8232-d6d6-5122-80a2-143130bc3231" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186476772}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "fb3352e8-034f-58da-933d-623fe3ee6c62" :question/math-academy-id "q-97756" :question/problem "Which of the following random variables can be modeled as a discrete uniform distribution?\n\nI. The number obtained when spinning a roulette wheel that is biased towards landing on $0.$\n\nII. The number obtained when picking a rational number between $0$ and $1$ at random.\n\nIII. The IQ of a randomly selected student." :question/worked-solution "We can use the discrete uniform distribution to model the outcome of a single event when the support $S$ is finite, and each $x\\in S$ is equally likely.\n\nWith that in mind, let's consider each of the given random variables.\n\n- Random variable I cannot be modeled as a discrete uniform distribution. Each outcome is not equally likely, because there is a higher probability of getting $0.$\n\n- Random variable II cannot be modeled as a discrete uniform distribution. This is because there are infinitely many rational numbers between $0$ and $1,$ and so the support is not finite.\n\n- Random variable III cannot be modeled as a discrete uniform distribution. Each outcome is not equally likely, because there are a small number of students with very low and very high IQs.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"None.\""} :step/id #uuid "c76af0ff-119c-5482-815e-41a5a57f8cc0" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186477002}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186477002 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186476936 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1036f694-7913-53be-8fe7-54060ff0f29c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186476229} {:answer/id #uuid "435a06fe-464c-5619-a04c-b90ad31d6ca1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186476349} {:answer/id #uuid "967ed33f-f676-5b92-aad4-1d38a4658979" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186476530} {:answer/id #uuid "97a49745-03be-5585-991c-e769b83f8c6f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186476534} {:answer/id #uuid "c81f6f7e-73b1-5545-9ab0-3dfc2ba67a9b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "None of the statements are true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186476653}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186476534} :answer-field/id #uuid "4036f65d-4a9f-5e2e-aa37-47bc9e7f7c6f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186476784}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "ab0e6acf-17b8-5853-9480-fac608a33b73" :question/math-academy-id "q-148230" :question/problem "Which of the following random variables can be modeled as a discrete uniform distribution?\n\nI. The outcome after tossing a fair coin.\n\nII. The number obtained when picking a real number between $2$ and $5$ at random.\n\nIII. The letter obtained when a letter from the word Mississippi is chosen at random." :question/worked-solution "We can use the discrete uniform distribution to model the outcome of a single event when the support $S$ is finite, and each $x\\in S$ is equally likely.\n\nWith that in mind, let's consider each of the given random variables.\n\n- Random variable I can be modeled as a discrete uniform distribution. There are a finite number of possible outcomes after tossing a coin: Head and tail, and since the coin is fair, each outcome has an equal probability of being selected.\n\n- Random variable II cannot be modeled as a discrete uniform distribution. This is because there are infinitely many real numbers between $2$ and $5,$ and so the support is not finite.\n\n- Random variable III cannot be modeled as a discrete uniform distribution. Each outcome is not equally likely, because so letters occur more often than others. For example, there is a lower probability of selecting the letter \"M\".\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\""} :step/id #uuid "fcd36363-9a82-5b2b-b02a-0de53a244207" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186476992}}] :activity/title "Modeling With Discrete Uniform Distributions" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186476202}] [{:activity/expected-seconds 420.0 :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172946} :activity/id #uuid "811b6c3b-bd9c-5c41-8fe9-0970ca3fedb9" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049433 :topic/id #uuid "f4f8286a-494e-5683-85be-d6899a52bb51" :topic/math-academy-id 3289} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172946 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186173179 :tutorial/content "The **gamma function** $\\Gamma(z)$ extends the factorial function to positive real numbers and parts of the complex plane.\n\nIn particular, for any natural number $n \\geq 1,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\Gamma(n) = (n-1)!\n$$\n\nIf the complex number $z$ satisfies $\\textrm{Re}(z) > 0,$ then the gamma function can be defined as the improper integral\n\n$$\n\\Gamma(z) = \\int_0^\\infty t^{z-1} e^{-t} \\, \\textrm dt.\n$$\n\nIn this lesson, we will only consider cases where $z$ is a positive real number." :tutorial/id #uuid "9fea375f-e55b-5c47-a7ae-c2ef9f860a2c" :tutorial/math-academy-id 5337 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "4bee0881-4e23-5850-9acc-e043caf9668c" :step/math-academy-id 33407 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172952}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172947 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172064 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186171870 :question/id #uuid "62da71f1-5a3a-509a-a68e-0596f88c4ae4" :question/math-academy-id "e-8951"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "59f9abf1-17db-5717-8660-a531ef00b15c" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049341}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172230} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172234} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186432691} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186432694} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186432696} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186432697} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186487302}] :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating the Gamma Function for a Positive Integer"} :step/id #uuid "96bce02d-680a-5dd7-a5f2-34c1e5553c65" :step/math-academy-id 33409 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172948}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172948 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186173180 :tutorial/content "To evaluate the gamma function at positive integer multiples of one-half, we use the following recursive formula:\n\n$$\n\\Gamma(z+1) = z \\Gamma(z), \\qquad \\Gamma \\left( \\dfrac{1}{2} \\right) = \\sqrt{\\pi}\n$$\n\nWe'll prove these results at the end of the lesson." :tutorial/id #uuid "a2e308ea-2921-5cda-bb43-b4914e8efaf1" :tutorial/math-academy-id 5338 :tutorial/title "The Recursive Formula and the Value of the Gamma Function at One-Half"} :step/id #uuid "5e5c30dd-3fe8-5761-b1e6-76ab3ced125d" :step/math-academy-id 33410 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172949}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172949 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172065 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186171871 :question/id #uuid "5d084f86-9874-5bdd-a416-dba742d7e4e5" :question/math-academy-id "e-8952"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "4c8de43f-dc61-5bbe-8b84-b8aa0be19146" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050380}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172231} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172235} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186432687} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186432689} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186432692} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186432695} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186487293} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186487388} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186487391}] :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating the Gamma Function at a Multiple of One-Half"} :step/id #uuid "bf112a76-b1a6-5da1-b844-9eb0f13eb3f1" :step/math-academy-id 33411 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186173037}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172950 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186173181 :tutorial/content "Previously, we used the following relationship between the gamma function and the factorial:\n\n$$\n\\Gamma(n) = (n-1)!\n$$\n\nThis relationship holds for any natural number $n \\geq 1.$ Now, let's prove it.\n\nFirst, we start with the following identity, which we proved earlier:\n\n$$\n\\Gamma(z+1) = z \\Gamma(z)\n$$\n\nFor any natural number $n \\geq 1,$ the above identity states the following:\n\n$$\n\\Gamma(n+1) = n \\Gamma(n)\n$$\n\nNow, let's repeatedly expand out the right-hand side, noticing that most of the terms can be grouped into a factorial:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Γ(n+1) & =nΓ(n) \\\\  & =n\\cdot \\overset{Γ(n)}{\\overset{⏞}{(n-1)\\cdot Γ(n-1)}} \\\\  & =n\\cdot (n-1)\\cdot \\overset{Γ(n-1)}{\\overset{⏞}{(n-2)\\cdot Γ(n-2)}} \\\\  & =… \\\\  & ={{n\\cdot (n-1)\\cdot (n-2)\\cdot …\\cdot 1}_{⏟}}_{n!}\\cdot Γ(1)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n\\Gamma(n+1) = n! \\cdot \\Gamma(1).\n$$\n\nTo evaluate $\\Gamma(1),$ we can use the definition of the gamma function:\n\n$$\n\\Gamma(1) = \\int_0^\\infty e^{-t} \\, \\textrm dt = 1\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\Gamma(n+1) = n!\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "66ce2df9-01d6-5873-b9e6-bb9e2a7d35af" :tutorial/math-academy-id 5339 :tutorial/title "Proving the Relationship Between the Gamma Function and the Factorial"} :step/id #uuid "272794b0-bed3-5ee3-a248-3dc313b1fd31" :step/math-academy-id 33412 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172951}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172951 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186173182 :tutorial/content "Previously, we used the fact that $\\Gamma \\left( \\dfrac{1}{2} \\right) = \\sqrt{\\pi}.$ To prove this, we can directly compute the value of $\\Gamma \\left( \\dfrac{1}{2} \\right)$ using the definition of the gamma function:\n\n$$\n\\Gamma\\left(\\dfrac{1}{2}\\right) = \\int_0^\\infty t^{-1/2} e^{-t} \\, \\text{d}t\n$$\n\nWe simplify this integral using the substitution $t = u^2,$ which implies $\\text{d}t = 2u \\, \\text{d}u.$ Also, note that $u \\to 0^+$ as $t \\to 0^+$ and $u \\to \\infty$ as $t \\to \\infty.$ So, we have\n\n$$\nt^{-1/2} e^{-t} \\, \\text{d}t = (u^2)^{-1/2} e^{-u^2} \\cdot 2u \\, \\text{d}u = e^{-u^2} \\cdot 2 \\, \\text{d}u.\n$$\n\nThus, we get\n\n$$\n\\Gamma\\left(\\dfrac{1}{2}\\right) = \\int_0^\\infty t^{-1/2} e^{-t} \\, \\text{d}t = 2 \\int_0^\\infty e^{-u^2} \\, \\text{d}u.\n$$\n\nThis is directly related to the well-known **Gaussian integral**:\n\n$$\n\\int_{-\\infty}^\\infty e^{-u^2} \\, \\text{d}u = \\sqrt{\\pi}\n$$\n\nSince the integrand $e^{-u^2}$ is even (i.e., symmetric about the $y$-axis), we have\n\n$$\n\\int_0^\\infty e^{-u^2} \\, \\text{d}u = \\dfrac{1}{2} \\int_{-\\infty}^\\infty e^{-u^2} \\, \\text{d}u = \\dfrac{1}{2} \\sqrt{\\pi}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting this result back into our expression for the Gamma function, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\Gamma\\left(\\dfrac{1}{2}\\right) = 2 \\cdot \\dfrac{1}{2} \\sqrt{\\pi} = \\sqrt{\\pi}.\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "79026717-bbea-524a-a1e0-01ef7570bfac" :tutorial/math-academy-id 5340 :tutorial/title "Evaluating the Gamma Function at One-Half"} :step/id #uuid "7a3ab1f8-c83c-5b32-87fd-ef21fb859957" :step/math-academy-id 33413} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172952 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172066 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186171872 :question/id #uuid "aab1e422-0ac4-59b7-bed9-5ce75b4fe22d" :question/math-academy-id "e-9033"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "7e75041c-d0be-5db2-a0db-7c2c2b57b44e" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048115}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172229} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172236} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186432686} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186432688} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186432690} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186432693} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186432698}] :knowledge-point/title "Defining the Gamma Function"} :step/id #uuid "09c6442a-deed-570b-8939-d804e74acc5c" :step/math-academy-id 33743 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172947}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186173037 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186173224 :tutorial/content "Here, we'll prove the recursive formula for the gamma function:\n\n$$\n\\Gamma(z+1) = z \\Gamma(z)\n$$\n\nThe formula comes from applying integration by parts to the integral in the definition of the gamma function. By definition,\n\n$$\n\\Gamma(z) = \\int_0^\\infty t^{z-1} e^{-t} \\, \\textrm dt, \\qquad\\textrm{Re}(z) > 0.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Γ(z+1) & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{t}^{z}{e}^{-t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =-{t}^{z}{e}^{-t}{|}_{0}^{\\infty }-{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }-z{t}^{z-1}{e}^{-t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={lim}_{t\\to \\infty }(-\\frac{{t}^{z}}{{e}^{t}})-{(-\\frac{{t}^{z}}{{e}^{t}})|}_{t=0}+{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }z{t}^{z-1}{e}^{-t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =0-0+z{{{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{t}^{z-1}{e}^{-t}\\,\\text{d}t}_{⏟}}_{Γ(z)} \\\\  & =zΓ(z)\\end{aligned}\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "68b4463b-d790-5bf1-9972-da35d3818a8b" :tutorial/math-academy-id 6699 :tutorial/title "Proof of the Recursive Formula"} :step/id #uuid "b3e9fad8-09f6-5769-9d53-ab348d94359e" :step/math-academy-id 40566 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172950}}] :activity/title "The Gamma Function" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186172142}] [{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046658} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186487412} :activity/id #uuid "364c21bd-4c9d-517c-904f-c5dd46711403" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049433 :topic/id #uuid "f4f8286a-494e-5683-85be-d6899a52bb51" :topic/math-academy-id 3289} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186487412 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172230 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a77bfb25-095b-5057-aba8-6f268f5f742f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "125" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186168244} {:answer/id #uuid "ae77212e-2a0c-5870-a919-ed167b91f235" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "25" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186168245} {:answer/id #uuid "ca6195c6-eda7-592f-badd-965c6e91f474" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186168246} {:answer/id #uuid "4275d640-6db8-51ce-bc7f-8b264307f6d1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "120" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186168247} {:answer/id #uuid "65656ef1-3e07-56e8-8556-fed7a19dd1fa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "24" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186168248}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186168248} :answer-field/id #uuid "a5b5e92c-1cc2-5285-b792-14856967269c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186168243}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "6f5a44a3-4ea9-54a0-a167-f690d2f22615" :question/math-academy-id "q-105736" :question/problem "Evaluate $Γ(5).$" :question/worked-solution "For any natural number $n\\ge 1,$ we have\n\n$$\nΓ(n)=(n-1)!.\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\nΓ(5)=4!=24.\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "2329b882-b9d8-579a-ac94-6f8fdcc055e1" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186487440}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186487416 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186487388 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3372a2b1-d89c-5ea5-ae2d-dc597ba5d5b8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{15\\sqrt{\\pi }}{7}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186486704} {:answer/id #uuid "580968ec-8d67-5eee-8ed5-dc153c668ede" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{17\\pi }{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186486787} {:answer/id #uuid "636f9395-da30-5934-b0e7-87be6c2499c2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{17\\sqrt{\\pi }}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186486812} {:answer/id #uuid "93cccf53-80db-5897-ab51-a8e524781991" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{15\\pi }{8}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186486919} {:answer/id #uuid "fa8079d4-cfe9-5db0-8ed9-f1196344fbb6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{17\\pi }{8}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186487130}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186486919} :answer-field/id #uuid "6aa87497-13d4-5b82-adae-cac5f16dbe31" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186487195}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "dcd3058f-0b72-5c3c-b3c8-705d9f7da27d" :question/math-academy-id "q-107857" :question/problem "Evaluate $\\sqrt{\\pi }\\,Γ(\\frac{7}{2}).$" :question/worked-solution "To evaluate the gamma function at multiples of one-half, we use the following recursive formula:\n\n$$\nΓ(z+1)=zΓ(z),\\,Γ(\\frac{1}{2})=\\sqrt{\\pi }\n$$\n\nSo, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{\\pi }\\,Γ(\\frac{7}{2}) & =\\sqrt{\\pi }\\cdot \\frac{5}{2}Γ(\\frac{5}{2}) \\\\  & =\\sqrt{\\pi }\\cdot \\frac{5}{2}\\cdot \\frac{3}{2}Γ(\\frac{3}{2}) \\\\  & =\\sqrt{\\pi }\\cdot \\frac{5}{2}\\cdot \\frac{3}{2}\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}Γ(\\frac{1}{2}) \\\\  & =\\sqrt{\\pi }\\cdot \\frac{5}{2}\\cdot \\frac{3}{2}\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}\\sqrt{\\pi } \\\\  & =\\frac{15\\pi }{8}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "32dda758-1c10-5e03-9107-ca2708d04732" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186487456}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186487438 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186487293 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "00754836-f0cd-50e5-aa02-7d189b80edae" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2\\sqrt{\\pi }" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186486596} {:answer/id #uuid "6bc959a4-5056-5435-b552-7ae39524df65" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "6\\sqrt{\\pi }" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186486827} {:answer/id #uuid "731dc778-4a1e-5474-afe6-d3d089ddf682" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3\\sqrt{\\pi }" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186486843} {:answer/id #uuid "7d43dd58-4629-52b1-8528-64bea432b08f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4\\sqrt{\\pi }" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186486866} {:answer/id #uuid "c7ee9600-62e5-57c5-a87c-047a37f4904a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "8\\sqrt{\\pi }" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186487037}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186486596} :answer-field/id #uuid "68ee2d15-0779-5b6d-a0a3-58d2b6c1ee1b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186487191}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "1397b57e-0338-5e52-920b-2086a4adac56" :question/math-academy-id "q-107861" :question/problem "$2\\,Γ(\\frac{5}{2})+Γ(\\frac{3}{2})=$" :question/worked-solution "To evaluate the gamma function at multiples of one-half, we use the following recursive formula:\n\n$$\nΓ(z+1)=zΓ(z),\\,Γ(\\frac{1}{2})=\\sqrt{\\pi }\n$$\n\nSo, we have the following:\n\n$$\nΓ(\\frac{3}{2})=\\frac{1}{2}Γ(\\frac{1}{2})=\\frac{1}{2}\\sqrt{\\pi },\n$$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Γ(\\frac{5}{2})=\\frac{3}{2}Γ(\\frac{3}{2})=\\frac{3}{2}\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}\\sqrt{\\pi }=\\frac{3}{4}\\sqrt{\\pi }\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n2\\,Γ(\\frac{5}{2})+Γ(\\frac{3}{2})=2\\cdot \\frac{3}{4}\\sqrt{\\pi }+\\frac{1}{2}\\sqrt{\\pi }=2\\sqrt{\\pi }.\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "c203f272-1a4b-54ae-b93f-7712948cad73"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186487440 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186487302 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "206fa060-e5b7-5b92-9899-8411d25fd4d2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "5\\,040" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186486659} {:answer/id #uuid "98d40466-0fee-5000-9a4e-74165fc45aba" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "720" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186486940} {:answer/id #uuid "9ff974d8-7678-510e-a50c-d042422993ff" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "40\\,320" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186486956} {:answer/id #uuid "c305fd9c-6218-528d-9a5f-49bc8d25aa53" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "10\\,080" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186487025} {:answer/id #uuid "e98f9aaf-ae44-57d2-b3aa-a7763efa9ced" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "120" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186487093}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186486659} :answer-field/id #uuid "22c5e926-ac1f-5f05-944d-e1b4e4b09520" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186487149}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "1f98d6c6-9c7c-5432-abf6-c7066e09f238" :question/math-academy-id "q-107851" :question/problem "Evaluate $Γ(8).$" :question/worked-solution "For any natural number $n\\ge 1,$ we have\n\n$$\nΓ(n)=(n-1)!.\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\nΓ(8)=7!=5\\,040.\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "cb0e3217-8975-5e32-8519-6af77622bccf" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186487416}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186487456 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186487391 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0998b024-6768-582f-945a-d5609ee9f1a1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186486614} {:answer/id #uuid "2cb28415-d03e-5dc3-8cb7-b89810a39b9f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186486680} {:answer/id #uuid "8908fc27-a7eb-5475-8abd-39bad6c188d9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{10}{\\sqrt{\\pi }}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186486891} {:answer/id #uuid "961cc474-dd53-5341-baee-0b7c843c8e47" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186486927} {:answer/id #uuid "f0a2c475-4407-520d-aa2c-30700dd13b24" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "5\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186487109}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186486680} :answer-field/id #uuid "58b55a76-73d7-558c-b98f-2fa3e367ce2b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186487179}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "df3869ef-efb9-5577-906d-3eff34aa9ab8" :question/math-academy-id "q-107859" :question/problem "Evaluate $\\frac{4}{\\sqrt{\\pi }}Γ(\\frac{5}{2}).$" :question/worked-solution "To evaluate the gamma function at multiples of one-half, we use the following recursive formula:\n\n$$\nΓ(z+1)=zΓ(z),\\,Γ(\\frac{1}{2})=\\sqrt{\\pi }\n$$\n\nSo, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{4}{\\sqrt{\\pi }}Γ(\\frac{5}{2}) & =\\frac{4}{\\sqrt{\\pi }}\\cdot \\frac{3}{2}Γ(\\frac{3}{2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{\\sqrt{\\pi }}\\cdot \\frac{3}{2}\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}\\cdot Γ(\\frac{1}{2}) \\\\  & =3\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "fd8c07fe-0842-5d46-b2b5-014a37a09eba" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186487438}}] :activity/title "The Gamma Function" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186486586}] [{:activity/expected-seconds 540.0 :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060552} :activity/id #uuid "29cf2698-b761-565d-9655-9c36bff61aab" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049805 :topic/id #uuid "155403ed-fbfa-59a9-b928-e762a73145ef" :topic/math-academy-id 247} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060552 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060984 :tutorial/content "The conditional statement $A \\Rightarrow B$ is logically equivalent to the disjunction $\\lnot A \\lor B{:}$ \n\n$$\nA \\Rightarrow B \\equiv \\lnot A \\lor B\n$$\n\nWe can verify this by the following truth table:\n\n| $\\,A\\,$ | $\\,B\\,$ | $\\, A \\Rightarrow B \\,$ | $\\, \\neg A \\lor B \\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\textrm T$ | $\\textrm T$ | $\\color{blue} \\textrm T$ | $\\color{blue} \\textrm T$ |\n| $\\textrm T$ | $\\textrm F$ | $\\color{blue} \\textrm F$ | $\\color{blue} \\textrm F$ |\n| $\\textrm F$ | $\\textrm T$ | $\\color{blue} \\textrm T$ | $\\color{blue} \\textrm T$ |\n| $\\textrm F$ | $\\textrm F$ | $\\color{blue} \\textrm T$ | $\\color{blue} \\textrm T$ |\n\nIn other words, any conditional statement of the form\n\n$\\qquad$ *if antecedent, then consequent*\n\ncan be expressed equivalently as \n\n$\\qquad$ *not antecedent, or consequent.*\n\nFor example, consider the following conditional statement:\n\n$\\qquad$ *If you do not take your watch, then you will be late for the meeting.* \n\nLet's rewrite this statement using its logically equivalent form. \n\nFirst, we denote the antecedent $A$ and consequent $B$ as follows:  \n\n- $A{:}$ You do not take your watch.\n\n- $B{:}$ You will be late for the meeting.\n\nThen, our initial statement can be written as $A \\Rightarrow B,$ which is logically equivalent to \n\n$$\n\\neg A \\lor B.\n$$\n\nThe negation of $A$ is:\n\n- $\\neg A{:}$ You take your watch.\n\nTherefore, the given statement is logically equivalent to the following:\n\n$\\qquad$ *You take your watch, or you will be late for the meeting.*\n\nWe can write this using more everyday language as follows:\n\n$\\qquad$ *Take your watch, or you will be late for the meeting.*\n\nAnother way to write this is to append the word \"either\" to the beginning of the sentence, like so:\n\n$\\qquad$ *Either you take your watch, or you will be late for the meeting.*\n\nUsing the word \"either\" helps clarify that the sentence structure presents two alternatives and is particularly useful when employing the disjunctive form $(\\lnot A\\lor B)$ in conversational or formal English. So, the phrase effectively conveys that one of the two stated conditions will hold true." :tutorial/id #uuid "6634a172-a5c9-557f-9091-35a4ebd198d3" :tutorial/math-academy-id 7978 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "082980d1-6f7a-54ee-8a3f-6ce0b731dc29" :step/math-academy-id 139680 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060553}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060553 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059687 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059409 :question/id #uuid "48364248-d296-502c-96da-d1905be35627" :question/math-academy-id "e-12707"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "8b60a8ae-c04f-530a-b89c-f8ac70969d0f" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048349} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048778} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050360}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060139} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060140} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186379181} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186379188} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186401722} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186401729} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186411060} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186411063}] :knowledge-point/title 